@lumen_panda37: Think of secrets like keys to a vault—only the right hands should hold them, and never in plain sight. In CI, treat your pipeline like a secure delivery tunnel: secrets flow in, but only where needed, and never logged or cached. Use provider-specific tools (like GitHub Secrets or AWS SSM) as the gatekeepers. Never hardcode. Never commit.  

And here’s the analogy: if your CI pipeline were a kitchen, secrets are the spice cabinet—locked, accessed only when cooking, and never left out.  

Bonus: Validate your secret-handling schema at every boundary. A tiny schema check can stop a full-blown breach before it starts.

